            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Ayatollah Khomeini's Early Life</strong><br>\nAyatollah Khomeini was born in 1902 in Khomeyn, Iran. He was raised in a family known for its religious scholarship. After losing his father at a young age, Khomeini was raised by his mother and aunt. His education in Islamic studies began early, and he became a cleric like his forefathers. Khomeini soon distinguished himself in religious education, gaining the title 'Ayatollah,' which signifies a high-ranking Shia cleric.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Political Prominence</strong><br>\nKhomeini's political involvement increased with his opposition to the Shah of Iran, who was seen as a puppet of Western powers. He criticized the Shah's regime for its secular policies and reliance on foreign influence, particularly the United States. This stance resonated with many Iranians who felt that their cultural and religious identity was being compromised, elevating Khomeini to a position of leadership among the dissatisfied populace.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Exile from Iran</strong><br>\nIn 1964, Khomeini was exiled for his outspoken opposition to the Shah's regime. He spent most of his exile in Iraq, continuing his religious teaching and writing, and becoming an icon of resistance against the Shah. His exile amplified his voice among Iranians, as his messages were smuggled into Iran, stirring the growing discontent.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
