          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Relationship with the West</strong><br>\nKhomeini's relationship with Western nations was marked by hostility. He viewed Western culture and political influence as a major threat to Islamic traditions and sovereignty. His rhetoric and policies often reflected a rejection of Westernization, aligning with a broader narrative of resistance in the Muslim world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Internal Policies and Human Rights</strong><br>\nDomestically, Khomeini's tenure saw the implementation of conservative Islamic laws, affecting personal freedoms and social norms. His government was also criticized for human rights abuses, including the persecution of political opponents and minorities. Despite this, he maintained a significant support base that endorsed his vision for an Islamic society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nKhomeini passed away in 1989, leaving behind a contentious legacy. He is revered by many Iranians as the leader who restored Islamic governance and independence. However, his critics remember his rule as authoritarian with a disregard for human rights. His influence on Iranian politics and society remains profound decades after his death.</p>\n"
              },
